Delty Full-Stack Take-Home Assignment
Build a small end-to-end web application with a React + TypeScript front end and a TypeScript back end, backed by a PostgreSQL database. The app is a chat experience powered by large language models and includes a document creation tool driven by the model.

This assignment is designed to be completed in ~2.5 hours. You are not expected to finish everything—we care more about how you prioritize, structure, and explain tradeoffs than about completeness.

You will have opportunities to go beyond the baseline if you choose.

Goals of the Assignment
We are evaluating:

• Your ability to design and ship a full-stack feature end-to-end
• Code quality, clarity, and structure
• Product and engineering judgment
• Practical operational awareness (streaming, failure handling, secrets, etc.)
• Communication of tradeoffs and decisions
• API design skills: designing a clean, unified interface for multiple model providers
Core Requirements (Tech Stack)
You must use:

• Frontend: React + TypeScript
• Backend: TypeScript (NestJS recommended, alternatives acceptable)
• Database: PostgreSQL
• Styling: Tailwind CSS (component libraries optional)
You may choose any supporting libraries or tools unless explicitly restricted.

The Two Big Things We Care About
A) Unified LLM Library (Non-negotiable)

You must build a small internal library (within the backend) that provides a single, unified interface to interact with different model providers.

Providers

Your library must include:

• Native integration with Anthropic + Gemini APIs
• A clear path to add the remaining provider (even if you do not fully implement it)
Bonus: implement all three providers natively (OpenAI + Anthropic + Gemini).

The unified interface should support

At minimum:

• Sending messages (system + user + assistant style messages)
• Streaming events (described below)
• Configuration knobs such as:
- model name
- temperature (or equivalent)
- max output tokens (or equivalent)
- thinking enabled by default, but configurable (on/off)
"Thinking" here means provider-specific "reasoning / thinking tokens." We do not care about the exact UI wording; we care that your library can surface this stream separately from normal text.

B) Real-time Streaming to the Frontend

Your backend must stream events to the frontend such that the UI can update live.

You must stream three kinds of information:

Text output stream (assistant content as it's generated)
Thinking stream (separate from the text stream)
Tool-use stream (the model deciding to call a tool, plus tool progress/results)
User Journeys
1) Chat

Build a chat screen where a user can:

• Type a message and receive a model response
• See the response appear as a stream, not only at the end
• See thinking appear as a separate streaming channel (for example, a collapsible panel, side rail, or inline "debug" area)
• See tool-use events stream in real time (for example, "Calling tool: createDocument", partial updates, completion)
Baseline scope: single conversation is fine.

Bonus: multiple chat threads (create, switch between, persist).

2) Document Tool (Tool Streaming Required)

Your chat must support a tool that creates or updates a document.

Example user prompts:

• "Create me a document with five different jokes."
• "Create a short report about large language models."
Requirements

• The model should be able to "call" a backend tool (for example createDocument or updateDocument)
• The frontend must show a document viewer/editor area
• As the tool runs, the document content must update in real time on the frontend (streamed updates)
Editor expectations

• You have freedom to choose the editor style (plain text, markdown viewer, minimal rich text, etc.)
• Baseline: the user does not need to manually edit the document (read-only is OK)
• Bonus: user editing + saving, or collaborative-feeling "live updates" while typing
Frontend Expectations
• Clean component structure and meaningful TypeScript types
• Reasonable user experience (loading, empty states, errors)
• A clear visualization of:
- streamed assistant text
- streamed thinking
- streamed tool events
• A document panel that updates live as the tool runs
Bonus Features (Pick Any Subset)
Bonus Tier 1: Product UX

• Multiple chat threads (list + switch + persist)
• Document update flows ("update the document to add 3 more new paragraphs")
Bonus Tier 2: Auth

• Sign up / sign in / sign out
Bonus Tier 3: Deployment

• Deploy to Google Cloud Platform or Amazon Web Services
• Provide at least one working URL
• Minimal infrastructure is fine
Deliverables
1) GitHub Repository

Include:

• Lightweight README with: how to run locally
• Source code for frontend + backend
2) Demo (Short)

A short demo video (2–4 minutes) walking through:

• Sending a message and watching text stream
• Showing thinking stream
• Triggering a document tool call and watching the document update live
• If implemented: threads, auth, deployment
3) Tradeoffs Document

Explain:

• Key technical decisions (especially the unified library interface and streaming design)
• What you prioritized and why
• What you intentionally didn't build
• Biggest risks/limitations
• What you would do next